# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres. SQLite was the engine until the move to Vercel — a serverless
# function has no writable disk, so a file-backed database cannot be read or
# written there. Any Postgres works; Neon and Supabase both have free tiers.
#
# The same URL is used locally. There is no SQLite fallback any more: running
# two engines means the case-sensitivity of `contains` differs between a
# developer's machine and production, which is exactly the class of bug that
# does not show up until somebody searches in lowercase.
#
# ── Pooled vs direct ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# A hosted Postgres behind a pooler needs two URLs, and they are not
# interchangeable. DATABASE_URL is the POOLED one (Supabase: port 6543) and
# carries every runtime query — serverless opens a connection per invocation,
# and a direct pool would be exhausted well before the traffic got interesting.
#
# DIRECT_URL is the unpooled one (Supabase: port 5432) and is used by
# `prisma migrate deploy` alone. Transaction-mode pooling cannot carry DDL,
# prepared statements or advisory locks, which is exactly what a migration
# needs, so migrations have to go around the pooler.
#
# The pooled URL must end with `pgbouncer=true`. Without it Prisma keeps using
# prepared statements, the pooler reuses a name across sessions, and you get
# intermittent `prepared statement "s0" already exists` — an error that shows up
# only under concurrency and looks like a random production fault.
#
# On a single unpooled Postgres (including the local one from `npm run db:local`)
# both may hold the same value.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:6543/postgres?sslmode=require&pgbouncer=true"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/postgres?sslmode=require"

# Optional. Without a key the map renders on MapLibre + OpenFreeMap.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_MAP_ID=

# ── Real station data ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Free key: https://openchargemap.org/site/develop/api
# With it set, "npm run import:stations" pulls real Pakistani charging
# stations into the database. Without it, stations are added by hand at
# /admin/stations. Nothing is ever generated — see scripts/import-stations.ts.
OPENCHARGEMAP_API_KEY=

# ── Sessions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signs every login cookie (drivers, business owners and operators).
# REQUIRED in production: without it nobody can sign in — the app fails
# closed rather than signing sessions with a guessable key. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# In local development only, ADMIN_PASSWORD is used if this is unset.
SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Admin portal ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   ENABLE_ADMIN    whether /admin exists at all. Enforced in middleware:
#                   anything other than exactly "true" makes every /admin URL
#                   a 404 identical to an unknown path.
#
# Who may use the portal is decided per account: sign in at /login with an
# account that has isAdmin set. Grant it with
#   npx tsx scripts/promote-admin.ts you@example.com
# and manage further operators from /admin/members. Every admin change is
# recorded at /admin/activity. There is no shared admin password any more.
#
#   ADMIN_PASSWORD  optional, local development only: the fallback signing key
#                   when SESSION_SECRET is unset. Ignored in production.
ENABLE_ADMIN=false
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Image uploads ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set automatically by Vercel when a Blob store is attached to the project.
# Leave it unset locally: without it, admin uploads write to public/uploads
# exactly as they always did, so the portal works on a developer machine with
# no cloud account. See src/lib/db/upload-actions.ts.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=""
